@* The grid's two decisions, both parameters: four columns at the widest breakpoint, and rows a
   little shorter than the default so a 2-row block reads as a block. Four cards fill both rows
   exactly — one 2×2, one half-width, two single cells — which is the point of a bento: the spans
   are chosen, not sprinkled, and a card still has no idea how many columns exist. *@
<BentoGrid Columns="4" RowClass="auto-rows-[120px]" Class="w-full">
    <BentoCard Class="lg:col-span-2 lg:row-span-2">
        <CardHeader>
            <CardTitle>Two by two</CardTitle>
            <CardDescription>
                The largest cell, and the only one that needed <code>row-span-2</code> as well as
                <code>col-span-2</code>.
            </CardDescription>
        </CardHeader>
    </BentoCard>

    <BentoCard Class="lg:col-span-2">
        <CardHeader>
            <CardTitle>Half a row</CardTitle>
            <CardDescription>Wide, but only one row tall.</CardDescription>
        </CardHeader>
    </BentoCard>

    <BentoCard>
        <CardHeader>
            <CardTitle>Single</CardTitle>
            <CardDescription>No span at all.</CardDescription>
        </CardHeader>
    </BentoCard>

    <BentoCard>
        <CardHeader>
            <CardTitle>Also single</CardTitle>
            <CardDescription>Rows are <code>auto-rows-[120px]</code>, not the default 180px.</CardDescription>
        </CardHeader>
    </BentoCard>
</BentoGrid>
